  "notes": [
    {
      "row": "notes",
      "title": "The order in one line",
      "text": "norm → ATTENTION → norm → add → norm → MLP → norm → add → ×layer_scalar. Four RMSNorms per layer. Wan and H3 both normalise only on the way in; Gemma brackets each sublayer on both sides.",
      "code": [
        {
          "role": "definition",
          "src": "gemma4.hf",
          "lines": [
            1365,
            1399
          ],
          "expect": {
            "symbol": "Gemma4TextDecoderLayer.forward",
            "first": "residual = hidden_states",
            "sha": "0caaedca",
            "span": [
              1354,
              1411
            ]
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "row": "notes",
      "title": "No conditioning signal at all",
      "text": "Compare the two video models: there, six AdaLN taps per block carry the timestep and scale or gate every sublayer. A language model has nothing to condition on — the residual adds are plain, and every layer treats every token the same way.",
      "code": [
        {
          "role": "definition",
          "src": "gemma4.hf",
          "lines": [
            1326,
            1337
          ],
          "expect": {
